WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.500
Every one of these worms kills the same 131 cells.

2
00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:08.860
How does a body lose that many cells without leaving a mess?

3
00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:14.520
In healthy tissue, dead cells are cleared so fast that you rarely see a corpse.

4
00:00:17.633 --> 00:00:19.073
Follow one doomed cell.

5
00:00:20.393 --> 00:00:22.833
Its own enzymes, called caspases, switch on.

6
00:00:24.033 --> 00:00:25.153
They cut other proteins.

7
00:00:26.413 --> 00:00:31.553
The cell shrinks, its chromatin packs down, its nucleus breaks into pieces, and its

8
00:00:31.553 --> 00:00:34.553
surface bubbles. But watch the membrane.

9
00:00:35.633 --> 00:00:36.933
It has not broken.

10
00:00:38.173 --> 00:00:43.113
The cell is taking itself apart, but for now every piece stays wrapped inside a

11
00:00:43.113 --> 00:00:48.073
closed membrane. This tidy, self-run death is called apoptosis.

12
00:00:49.183 --> 00:00:51.803
While still sealed, it can send two signals.

13
00:00:53.123 --> 00:00:57.423
First, a channel in its membrane opens, and small molecules puff out.

14
00:00:58.623 --> 00:01:00.063
They are a find-me signal.

15
00:01:01.263 --> 00:01:03.963
They call a phagocyte, a cell that eats other cells.

16
00:01:05.033 --> 00:01:10.653
Second, a lipid called phosphatidylserine normally sits mostly on the membrane's

17
00:01:10.653 --> 00:01:16.673
inner face. The caspases cut the membrane proteins that control which face it sits

18
00:01:16.673 --> 00:01:20.013
on. Now it appears on the outer face.

19
00:01:21.153 --> 00:01:22.433
That is the eat-me flag.

20
00:01:23.483 --> 00:01:25.203
Here is the idea to carry.

21
00:01:26.483 --> 00:01:30.443
In apoptosis, the membrane holds while the cell takes itself apart.

22
00:01:31.743 --> 00:01:34.863
That buys time for a phagocyte to eat it before anything leaks.

23
00:01:35.967 --> 00:01:39.147
The phagocyte arrives and recognises the flag.

24
00:01:40.347 --> 00:01:44.247
In the simplest picture, it wraps around the whole cell and swallows it.

25
00:01:45.507 --> 00:01:47.007
A lysosome digests it.

26
00:01:48.167 --> 00:01:52.167
Nothing spills. That is why you rarely see a corpse.

27
00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:57.790
Now put the same kind of dying cell in a dish, where no phagocyte will come.

28
00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:02.630
Time passes. What happens to its membrane?

29
00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:14.380
Often, it fails. The membrane that held through the early stages now leaks, and the

30
00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:18.920
contents spill. This late leak is called secondary necrosis.

31
00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:23.180
In a tissue, a phagocyte usually gets there first.

32
00:02:24.233 --> 00:02:26.253
Some cells die the other way round.

33
00:02:27.493 --> 00:02:30.193
This one swells, and its membrane tears early.

34
00:02:31.513 --> 00:02:34.573
Its contents spill at once, including alarm signals.

35
00:02:35.933 --> 00:02:38.953
These can call in the immune system and cause inflammation.

36
00:02:40.473 --> 00:02:42.293
This messy death is called necrosis.

37
00:02:43.467 --> 00:02:48.207
Tearing does not mean accident: some tearing deaths are genetic programs too.

38
00:02:49.507 --> 00:02:51.987
In its simplest form, the difference is timing.

39
00:02:53.387 --> 00:02:55.847
Apoptosis keeps the membrane intact early.

40
00:02:57.287 --> 00:02:59.227
Necrotic deaths generally open it early.

41
00:03:00.367 --> 00:03:01.667
So back to the worm.

42
00:03:02.987 --> 00:03:07.147
Its doomed cells are usually eaten by neighbouring cells before they spill.

43
00:03:08.407 --> 00:03:12.307
A membrane that holds until the meal often makes for a quieter death.
